    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-4401",
      "problem": "Find $AB$ and $BA,$ if \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14273053/assets/f680335355447c50c5ec3a3543ae2933691ef7553237713d31fe5af38bf7fbf2.svg)\nand\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14273053/assets/6b6ad8f45c73f943d7590513b9a5173d32ddd61b51d418608f74ae32dc3c19bf.svg)",
      "worked_solution": "Multiplying each row in the first matrix by each column in the second, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1\\cdot 0+0\\cdot (-1) & 1\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 2 \\\\ -5\\cdot 0+1\\cdot (-1) & -5\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BA & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-5) & 0\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 1 \\\\ -1\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-5) & -1\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ -11 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** Notice that \n\n$$\nAB \\neq BA.\n$$\n\nMatrix multiplication is not **commutative**, meaning that when we switch the order of multiplication, we often get a different result (like we did in this example).",
